    "greetings": "💡 <strong>Selamat</strong> is the all-purpose greeting prefix. Locals will always appreciate you trying a few words of Malay.",
    "food": "💡 Malaysian food is often served family-style. Nasi lemak (coconut rice with sambal) is the national dish — available everywhere, even for breakfast.",
    "directions": "💡 Grab is the easiest way around KL. Just show the driver your destination on the screen.",
    "numbers": "💡 Malay numbers are logical — \"dua puluh lima\" = 25 (two tens five). Easy to learn.",
    "emergency": "💡 Emergency in Malaysia: <strong>999</strong> for police, fire, and ambulance.",
    "shopping": "Bargain at a pasar malam, not in a mall. Start below the asking price.",
    "medical": "Drink bottled or boiled water. Call 999 for an ambulance.",
    "immigration": "Most visitors are stamped in for 30–90 days. Keep the arrival record.",
    "hotel": "Take your shoes off if your host does. Save the address in Grab before you land.",
    "transport": "Use Grab rather than hailing a taxi. The LRT covers central Kuala Lumpur."
  },
  "pronGuide": {
    "title": "Malay Sounds",
    "tips": [
      "Pronunciation is mostly phonetic — say it as it looks",
      "C is always \"ch\" (as in <em>cinta</em> = chin-tah)",
      "Stress usually falls on the second-to-last syllable",
      "NG at the start of a word sounds like singing (ng-ah = yes)"
    ]
  },
